public_system.narrative:
Indonesia's public system rests on national health insurance (JKN), run by the state-owned insurer BPJS Kesehatan, which covered 282.7 million people, more than 98 percent of the population, at the end of 2025, although only 81.45 percent of members were active.{{1}}{{2}} The World Bank reports that health service visits under the scheme grew from 378 million in 2020 to 686 million in 2024, and that out-of-pocket spending fell from 45 to 31 percent of total health spending.{{3}}

Care is delivered through more than 10,000 community health centers (puskesmas){{7}} and through hospitals run by the central government, local governments and private operators.{{4}} The Health Ministry has activated 300,000 community health posts (posyandu) and in 2023 began building vertical hospitals for cancer, heart, stroke and kidney care in Surabaya, Makassar, the new national capital in East Kalimantan and Jayapura.{{5}}

The free health check program began on 10 February 2025 at about 10,200 puskesmas.{{6}} It screened 70 million people in 2025 and another 73.8 million in 2026 up to 31 July, against a 2026 target of 130 million.{{7}} From 2027 to 2029 the government plans to renovate 8,001 puskesmas, build 1,988 new ones and upgrade 441 regional hospitals.{{8}}
